WF-75 — Window-Closing Nudge (scheduled)

## Summary

- Trigger: Schedule — every 2 hours. Project's first scheduled/background workflow (WF-7x range,
  pulled forward from post-go-live per user decision 2026-06-08).
- Purpose (admin-facing, advisory-only): when a customer is mid-consultation and their 24h WhatsApp
  free-reply window is nearing close (last customer inbound 18–24h ago) AND Dr. Chinmay has not yet
  replied to that inbound, post a non-blocking heads-up into the consult channel so he can answer
  inside the window (free, full-fidelity text) before the relay falls back to a charged template
  (PDF-15 / DD-2 is the backstop if he chooses to ignore it).
- Outputs: 0..N advisory Slack messages (one per matching consultation), each via WF-51 to the
  customer's consult channel. No customer contact, no auto-reply, no DB write, never blocks.
- State Transitions: none.
- Calls Sub-Workflows: WF-51 (Send Slack Message).
- Design source: DD-5 / DD-F (`docs/artefacts/specs/2026-06-08-24h-window-deliverability-design.md`
  + `state.md` PDF-18). Window source per DD-B = `chinmay_astro.messages` (NOT `users.last_message_at`,
  which the relay path never writes → stale).

## Inputs

### Canonical (required)
- none — Schedule trigger fires on a fixed cadence (every 2h); the workflow reads all state from the DB.

## Notes

- **Window-state source = `chinmay_astro.messages`, WhatsApp-scoped via `metadata->>'transport'='wa'`
  (DD-B + build correction 2026-06-09; transport discriminator adopted PDF-20 2026-06-09).**
  The `messages` log mixes transports: a customer's WhatsApp message is `direction='inbound'`
  `metadata.transport='wa'`; Dr. Chinmay's own typing in Slack is logged `direction='inbound'`
  `transport='slack'` (`message_type='slack_text'`); this workflow's own nudge is logged
  `direction='outbound'` `transport='slack'`. So both halves of the "unanswered" test MUST be
  WhatsApp-scoped on transport:
    - customer's last inbound = `MAX(created_at) WHERE direction='inbound' AND metadata->>'transport'='wa'`
    - Dr. Chinmay answered = a WhatsApp send to the customer = `direction='outbound' AND metadata->>'transport'='wa'`
  Filtering on `transport='wa'` (NOT a `message_type` allow-list) is the correct discriminator: the 24h
  window re-opens on ANY customer WhatsApp inbound — text, interactive, OR media — so `transport='wa'`
  counts them all, whereas `message_type IN ('text','interactive')` would under-count a future media
  inbound. Excluding `transport='slack'` on the outbound side is what lets the nudge REPEAT (~3–4× across
  the 18→24h stretch) instead of self-disabling the moment its own Slack post lands; excluding it on the
  inbound side keeps the astrologer's Slack typing from masquerading as a fresh customer message.
  `transport` is non-null on every row (WF-60 defaults it to 'wa'), verified live 2026-06-09.
- **Divergence from PDF-15 (WF-41 `Load Last Inbound`):** PDF-15 reads window state with a looser
  `direction='inbound'` (no transport scope). That is acceptable for its single-user relay-time read but
  is a latent skew (a Slack-inbound row could read as the customer's last inbound). Logged as an adjacent
  finding in this sprint's `followups.md` for separate triage — NOT changed by this item.
- **24h boundary computed in SQL** (timestamptz vs `NOW()`), TZ-correct, no dependency on n8n runtime TZ —
  same discipline as PDF-15's WF-41 query.
- **Repeat + self-termination (DD-F):** every 2h poll re-evaluates; a user keeps matching (and re-nudging)
  while last-inbound is 18–24h old and unanswered. Self-terminates two ways: (1) Dr. Chinmay replies on
  WhatsApp → `last_outbound_wa > last_inbound` → unanswered clause false; (2) last-inbound passes 24h →
  out of the 18–24h band → no longer selected (window already closed; relay now goes template/charged, so
  the nudge has no purpose).

## Algorithm

Step 1: Schedule Trigger — fires every 2 hours. No input payload.

Step 2: Load Window-Closing Consults (Postgres, executeQuery, alwaysOutputData=true). Single scan
  returning one row per customer who needs a nudge this cycle:

      SELECT u.id                AS user_id,
             u.name              AS user_name,
             u.slack_channel_id  AS slack_channel_id,
             u.current_consultation_id AS consultation_id,
             agg.last_inbound    AS last_inbound,
             GREATEST(0, CEIL(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (agg.last_inbound + INTERVAL '24 hours' - NOW())) / 3600))::int AS hours_until_close
      FROM chinmay_astro.users u
      JOIN LATERAL (
        SELECT
          MAX(created_at) FILTER (WHERE direction='inbound'  AND metadata->>'transport'='wa') AS last_inbound,
          MAX(created_at) FILTER (WHERE direction='outbound' AND metadata->>'transport'='wa') AS last_outbound_wa
        FROM chinmay_astro.messages m
        WHERE m.user_id = u.id
      ) agg ON true
      WHERE u.status = 'consultation_active'
        AND u.slack_channel_id IS NOT NULL
        AND agg.last_inbound IS NOT NULL
        AND agg.last_inbound <= NOW() - INTERVAL '18 hours'
        AND agg.last_inbound >= NOW() - INTERVAL '24 hours'
        AND (agg.last_outbound_wa IS NULL OR agg.last_inbound > agg.last_outbound_wa)

  → 0 rows: alwaysOutputData=true emits an empty item; downstream Code emits nothing → no nudges. Go Step 3.
  → N rows: N items, one per matching consultation. Go Step 3.

Step 3: Build Nudge Payload (Code, runs over all input items). For each row that carries a real
  `slack_channel_id`, emit one WF-51 contract item:
      { channelId: <slack_channel_id>,
        messageText: "⏳ Heads-up: <user_name>'s free-reply window closes in ~<hours_until_close>h. "
                   + "Reply on WhatsApp within the window to answer for free in plain text — after it "
                   + "closes, your reply goes out as a template. Ignore this if you are done.",
        userId: <user_id>,            // forwarded to WF-51 → WF-60 for clean attribution
        consultationId: <consultation_id> }
  Rows with no `slack_channel_id` or no `user_id` are skipped (defensive — the SQL already filters
  channel-null, so this is belt-and-braces). If the input is the single empty 0-row item, return []
  (no items) so Step 4 does not fire. Go Step 4.

Step 4: Call WF-51 (Send Slack Message), mode=each — fires once per payload item (one Slack post per
  consultation). WF-51's entry guard requires `{channelId, messageText}`; both are always present on
  every emitted item. No output consumed. End.

## Outputs

- 0..N advisory Slack messages posted to consult channels (one per matching open consultation this cycle).
- No customer-facing message, no DB write, no state transition. Harmless to ignore; safe to run on an
  empty match set.
